## JPA Index Annotations (For Schema Documentation)

```java
@Entity
@Table(
    name = "orders",
    indexes = {
        @Index(name = "idx_orders_status", columnList = "status"),
        @Index(name = "idx_orders_user_id_status", columnList = "user_id, status"),
        @Index(name = "idx_orders_created_at", columnList = "created_at DESC")
    }
)
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "user_id")  // FK column — should be indexed
    private User user;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    private OrderStatus status;

    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
}
```

## Flyway Migration (Production Index Management)

```sql
-- V5__add_order_indexes.sql
-- Always use CONCURRENTLY in PostgreSQL to avoid table lock
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_status ON orders(status);
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id_status ON orders(user_id, status);
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_created_at ON orders(created_at DESC);

-- Covering index: includes all columns the query needs
-- SELECT id, status FROM orders WHERE user_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_covering
  ON orders(user_id, created_at DESC)
  INCLUDE (id, status);  -- PostgreSQL covering index
```

## EXPLAIN ANALYZE Output

```sql
-- Before index:
EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 42 AND status = 'PENDING';
/*
Seq Scan on orders  (cost=0.00..48502.50 rows=1 width=128)
  Filter: ((user_id = 42) AND (status = 'PENDING'))
  Rows Removed by Filter: 999999
Execution Time: 185.432 ms   ← Sequential scan on 1M rows!
*/

-- After index on (user_id, status):
EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = 42 AND status = 'PENDING';
/*
Index Scan using idx_orders_user_id_status on orders
  (cost=0.43..8.45 rows=1 width=128)
  Index Cond: ((user_id = 42) AND (status = 'PENDING'))
Execution Time: 0.234 ms   ← 800x faster!
*/
```

## Composite Index Column Order

```sql
-- Index: (user_id, status)
-- CAN use this index:
  WHERE user_id = 42                   -- leading column prefix
  WHERE user_id = 42 AND status = 'P'  -- full index

-- CANNOT use this index:
  WHERE status = 'PENDING'             -- doesn't start with user_id

-- Rule: most selective AND most commonly queried column first
-- user_id has high cardinality (many distinct values) → good leading column
-- status has low cardinality (few values) → better as second column
```

## Index on Derived/Expression Columns

```sql
-- Query: WHERE LOWER(email) = ?
-- Normal index on email won't work for case-insensitive search!
CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users(LOWER(email));

-- Spring Data:
@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE LOWER(u.email) = LOWER(:email)")
Optional<User> findByEmailIgnoreCase(@Param("email") String email);
-- Now uses the expression index
```

## Production Reality

Check `pg_stat_user_indexes` (PostgreSQL) for unused indexes — they cost writes with no benefit. Common mistake: adding indexes for every possible filter column without checking whether those queries actually run and whether the table is large enough for an index to matter. Tables under 10,000 rows often do full scans faster than index scans due to overhead.

## JPQL - Entity Model Queries

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    // JPQL: operates on entity names and field names
    @Query("SELECT o FROM Order o "
         + "JOIN FETCH o.user u "
         + "WHERE o.status = :status "
         + "AND o.createdAt > :since "
         + "ORDER BY o.createdAt DESC")
    List<Order> findRecentByStatus(
        @Param("status") OrderStatus status,
        @Param("since") LocalDateTime since
    );

    // JPQL aggregation
    @Query("SELECT o.status, COUNT(o), SUM(o.totalAmount) "
         + "FROM Order o "
         + "GROUP BY o.status")
    List<Object[]> getOrderSummaryByStatus();

    // Named JPQL reference
    @Query("SELECT new com.example.dto.UserOrderCount(u.id, u.name, COUNT(o)) "
         + "FROM Order o JOIN o.user u "
         + "GROUP BY u.id, u.name "
         + "HAVING COUNT(o) > :minCount")
    List<UserOrderCount> findActiveUsers(@Param("minCount") long minCount);
}
```

## Native SQL - DB-Specific Features

```java
// Window functions — not in JPQL
@Query(
    value = "SELECT o.id, o.user_id, o.total_amount, "
          + "  RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY o.user_id ORDER BY o.total_amount DESC) as rank "
          + "FROM orders o "
          + "WHERE rank <= 3",  -- Not valid in PostgreSQL (would need subquery)
    nativeQuery = true
)
List<Object[]> findTopOrdersPerUser();

// CTE (Common Table Expressions)
@Query(
    value = "WITH monthly_totals AS ( "
          + "  SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) as month, "
          + "         SUM(total_amount) as total "
          + "  FROM orders "
          + "  WHERE created_at > NOW() - INTERVAL '12 months' "
          + "  GROUP BY 1 "
          + ") "
          + "SELECT * FROM monthly_totals ORDER BY month DESC",
    nativeQuery = true
)
List<Object[]> getLast12MonthsRevenue();

// PostgreSQL upsert
@Modifying
@Query(
    value = "INSERT INTO product_stock (product_id, quantity) "
          + "VALUES (:productId, :quantity) "
          + "ON CONFLICT (product_id) "
          + "DO UPDATE SET quantity = excluded.quantity",
    nativeQuery = true
)
void upsertStock(
    @Param("productId") Long productId,
    @Param("quantity") int quantity
);
```

## Mapping Native Query Results

```java
// To an interface projection
public interface OrderSummary {
    Long getId();
    String getUserName();
    BigDecimal getTotalAmount();
}

@Query(
    value = "SELECT o.id, u.name as user_name, o.total_amount "
          + "FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id",
    nativeQuery = true
)
List<OrderSummary> findOrderSummaries();
// Column aliases must match interface method names (camelCase → snake_case mapped)

// To a SqlResultSetMapping for entities
@SqlResultSetMapping(
    name = "OrderUserMapping",
    classes = @ConstructorResult(
        targetClass = OrderDTO.class,
        columns = {
            @ColumnResult(name = "id", type = Long.class),
            @ColumnResult(name = "user_name", type = String.class)
        }
    )
)
```

## Criteria API (Dynamic Queries)

```java
// For dynamic WHERE clause construction
public List<Order> searchOrders(OrderSearchCriteria criteria) {
    CriteriaBuilder cb = em.getCriteriaBuilder();
    CriteriaQuery<Order> query = cb.createQuery(Order.class);
    Root<Order> order = query.from(Order.class);

    List<Predicate> predicates = new ArrayList<>();
    if (criteria.getStatus() != null)
        predicates.add(cb.equal(order.get("status"), criteria.getStatus()));
    if (criteria.getUserId() != null)
        predicates.add(cb.equal(order.get("user").get("id"), criteria.getUserId()));
    if (criteria.getFromDate() != null)
        predicates.add(cb.greaterThan(order.get("createdAt"), criteria.getFromDate()));

    query.where(predicates.toArray(new Predicate[0]));
    return em.createQuery(query).getResultList();
}
// Or use Spring Data Specifications (cleaner API)
```

## Production Reality

For complex reporting endpoints (dashboards, exports, analytics), don't hesitate to write native SQL. A perfectly optimized SQL query using window functions, CTEs, and database-specific hints will outperform the equivalent JPQL — and reporting queries don't need database portability. Keep native queries in the repository interface with clear naming conventions so they're easy to find.

## Offset Pagination — Performance Problem

```java
// Spring Data Pageable
Page<Order> page = orderRepo.findByStatus(status, PageRequest.of(500, 20));
// Executes:
// SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = ? ORDER BY id LIMIT 20 OFFSET 10000;
// + separately:
// SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE status = ?;

// Problem at page 500:
// DB: scan rows 1-10000, discard, return 10001-10020
// Gets slower with each page
```

## Slice<T> — Skip the COUNT Query

```java
// Returns Slice instead of Page — no COUNT(*) query
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    Slice<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status, Pageable pageable);
    // Only: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = ? LIMIT 21 OFFSET ?
    // Fetches N+1 to know if there's a next page
    // hasNext() available, but no totalPages or totalElements
}

// Use when you don't need total count (infinite scroll, load more):
Slice<Order> slice = orderRepo.findByStatus(status, PageRequest.of(page, 20));
boolean hasMore = slice.hasNext();
List<Order> orders = slice.getContent();
```

## Keyset Pagination (Cursor-Based)

```java
// Repository:
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o "
     + "WHERE o.status = :status AND o.id > :lastId "
     + "ORDER BY o.id ASC")
List<Order> findNextPage(
    @Param("status") OrderStatus status,
    @Param("lastId") Long lastId,
    Pageable pageable
);
// Pageable used only for LIMIT — OFFSET is not used here!

// Service:
public OrderPage getNextPage(OrderStatus status, Long lastId, int size) {
    List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findNextPage(
        status,
        lastId,    // Cursor from last page
        PageRequest.of(0, size + 1)  // Fetch N+1 to check hasNext
    );
    boolean hasNext = orders.size() > size;
    if (hasNext) orders = orders.subList(0, size);

    Long nextCursor = hasNext ? orders.get(orders.size() - 1).getId() : null;
    return new OrderPage(orders, nextCursor, hasNext);
}

// SQL generated:
// SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING' AND id > 10000 ORDER BY id LIMIT 21
// Uses index on id — instant, regardless of position
```

## Multi-Column Keyset (Created-At + ID)

```java
// When ordering by created_at (non-unique):
@Query("SELECT o FROM Order o "
     + "WHERE o.status = :status "
     + "AND (o.createdAt < :lastCreatedAt "
     +   "OR (o.createdAt = :lastCreatedAt AND o.id < :lastId)) "
     + "ORDER BY o.createdAt DESC, o.id DESC")
List<Order> findNextPageByDate(
    @Param("status") OrderStatus status,
    @Param("lastCreatedAt") LocalDateTime lastCreatedAt,
    @Param("lastId") Long lastId,
    Pageable pageable
);
// Handles tie-breaking when multiple rows have same created_at
// Requires composite index on (status, created_at, id)
```

## COUNT Query Optimization for Page<T>

```java
// Custom count query for Page<T> — avoids expensive JOIN in count
@Query(
    value = "SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.user WHERE o.status = :status",
    countQuery = "SELECT COUNT(o) FROM Order o WHERE o.status = :status"
    // Count query doesn't need JOIN FETCH — faster
)
Page<Order> findByStatusWithUser(
    @Param("status") OrderStatus status,
    Pageable pageable
);
```

## Production Reality

For APIs with infinite scroll (mobile apps, social feeds): use keyset pagination — it's the only approach that scales. For admin UIs where users need to jump to specific pages: offset pagination with `Slice` (no count) is acceptable if you limit max pages. Never run offset pagination without a cap — `Pageable pageable` with no max offset validation is a DoS vector.

## What readOnly Actually Does

```
@Transactional(readOnly = true) effects:

1. Hibernate behavior:
   - Skips entity snapshot creation on load
   - Skips dirty checking at flush time
   - FlushMode set to MANUAL (no auto-flush before queries)
   - Entities loaded in "read-only" mode — cannot be "dirtied"

2. Spring transaction manager:
   - Sets Connection.setReadOnly(true) on JDBC connection
   - Some connection pools route to read replica

3. Database effects (varies by DB/driver):
   - PostgreSQL: may skip row-level lock acquisition for SELECT
   - MySQL: no action (ignores hint)
   - Some proxies (ProxySQL, pgBouncer): route to read replica
```

## Class-Level Default Pattern

```java
@Service
@Transactional(readOnly = true)  // Default: all methods are read-only
public class OrderService {

    public List<Order> findAll() {             // READ — readOnly ✓
        return orderRepo.findAll();
    }

    public Optional<Order> findById(Long id) { // READ — readOnly ✓
        return orderRepo.findById(id);
    }

    @Transactional  // Override: write operation needs full tx
    public Order createOrder(CreateOrderRequest req) {
        // This method gets full transaction with dirty checking
        return orderRepo.save(new Order(req));
    }

    @Transactional  // Override for write
    public void cancelOrder(Long id) {
        Order order = orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        order.setStatus(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);
    }
}
```

## Memory Impact on Bulk Reads

```java
// Without readOnly: 10,000 orders loaded = 10,000 original snapshots stored
@Transactional
public void generateReport() {
    List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll();  // 10,000 orders
    // Hibernate stores: 10,000 Order objects + 10,000 state snapshots
    // Memory: ~2x the entity objects
    orders.forEach(this::writeToReport);  // dirty check at end: 10,000 comparisons
}

// With readOnly: no snapshots, no dirty check
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public void generateReport() {
    List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll();  // 10,000 orders
    // Hibernate stores: 10,000 Order objects, NO snapshots
    // Memory: 1x
    // No dirty check on completion
    orders.forEach(this::writeToReport);
}
```

## Read Replica Routing

```java
// With routing data source setup:
@Bean
public DataSource dataSource() {
    AbstractRoutingDataSource routingDS = new AbstractRoutingDataSource() {
        @Override
        protected Object determineCurrentLookupKey() {
            return TransactionSynchronizationManager.isCurrentTransactionReadOnly()
                ? "read-replica"
                : "primary";
        }
    };
    routingDS.setTargetDataSources(Map.of(
        "primary", primaryDataSource(),
        "read-replica", replicaDataSource()
    ));
    routingDS.setDefaultTargetDataSource(primaryDataSource());
    return routingDS;
}
// @Transactional(readOnly = true) → routes to replica automatically!
```

## Write Attempt on ReadOnly Transaction

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public Order accidentalWrite(Long id) {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    order.setStatus(OrderStatus.SHIPPED);  // Try to modify
    return order;
    // Hibernate: entity is in read-only mode — change is IGNORED
    // No UPDATE is executed — silently discarded!
    // OR: driver throws exception if Connection is truly read-only
}
// This is why readOnly is not just documentation — it prevents accidental writes
```

## Production Reality

Annotate all service classes with `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` as the default. This ensures every query method automatically benefits without per-method annotations. Most production issues with readOnly are: (1) forgetting to override with `@Transactional` on write methods — causing silent data loss, or (2) not having read replicas, making the routing benefit moot. Profile your specific workload to quantify the dirty-checking benefit.

## @Modifying for Bulk Updates

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    // Direct UPDATE — no entity loading
    @Modifying
    @Query("UPDATE Order o SET o.status = :newStatus "
         + "WHERE o.status = :oldStatus AND o.createdAt < :before")
    int bulkUpdateStatus(
        @Param("newStatus") OrderStatus newStatus,
        @Param("oldStatus") OrderStatus oldStatus,
        @Param("before") LocalDateTime before
    );
    // Returns: number of rows affected
    // Executes: UPDATE orders SET status = ? WHERE status = ? AND created_at < ?
    // One SQL statement — doesn't matter if 1 or 1,000,000 rows

    // Direct DELETE — no entity loading
    @Modifying
    @Query("DELETE FROM Order o WHERE o.status = 'CANCELLED' AND o.createdAt < :before")
    int deleteOldCancelledOrders(@Param("before") LocalDateTime before);
}

// Service usage:
@Transactional
public void archiveOldOrders() {
    int archived = orderRepo.bulkUpdateStatus(
        OrderStatus.ARCHIVED,
        OrderStatus.COMPLETED,
        LocalDateTime.now().minusMonths(6)
    );
    log.info("Archived {} orders", archived);
}
```

## clearAutomatically After Bulk Operations

```java
@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true, flushAutomatically = true)
@Query("UPDATE Order o SET o.status = :status")
void updateAllStatus(@Param("status") OrderStatus status);

// clearAutomatically: clears persistence context after execution
// Prevents stale entity state if you load entities after the bulk update
// Without it: entities loaded before the bulk update still show old values

@Transactional
public void processOrders() {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
    System.out.println(order.getStatus());  // PENDING

    orderRepo.updateAllStatus(OrderStatus.SHIPPED);  // Bulk update
    // clearAutomatically = true → PC cleared

    // Now re-load:
    order = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();  // Fresh DB read
    System.out.println(order.getStatus());  // SHIPPED
}
```

## Bulk Deletes — deleteAllInBatch vs deleteAll

```java
// deleteAll() — BAD for large collections
orderRepo.deleteAll();
// Executes:
// 1. SELECT * FROM orders   ← loads EVERYTHING into memory
// 2. DELETE FROM orders WHERE id = 1  ← for each entity
// 3. DELETE FROM orders WHERE id = 2
// ... N queries total

// deleteAllInBatch() — GOOD
orderRepo.deleteAllInBatch();
// Executes:
// DELETE FROM orders WHERE id IN (1, 2, 3, ...) ← one statement

// deleteAllByIdInBatch() — most efficient for known IDs
List<Long> ids = Arrays.asList(1L, 2L, 3L);
orderRepo.deleteAllByIdInBatch(ids);
// DELETE FROM orders WHERE id IN (1, 2, 3)
```

## Batch INSERT with saveAll()

```java
// For batch inserts, configure:
# application.properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_inserts=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_updates=true

// In service:
@Transactional
public void bulkCreateOrders(List<CreateOrderRequest> requests) {
    List<Order> orders = requests.stream()
        .map(Order::new)
        .collect(toList());
    orderRepo.saveAll(orders);
    // With batch_size=50: 1000 orders = 20 batch INSERT statements
    // Without: 1000 individual INSERT statements
}

// IMPORTANT: IDENTITY generation disables batch INSERT!
// Use SEQUENCE strategy for batch inserts:
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "order_seq")
@SequenceGenerator(name = "order_seq", allocationSize = 50)
private Long id;
// allocationSize=50 pre-allocates 50 IDs — enables batch INSERT
```

## Production Reality

For ETL jobs, data migrations, and cleanup tasks: always use `@Modifying @Query` for bulk operations. The difference between `deleteAll()` and `@Modifying DELETE` on a million-row table is the difference between an OOM crash and a 100ms query. Also: never call bulk delete operations in the same transaction where you've loaded those entities — clear the PC first or use `clearAutomatically`.
